# PingOne Privilege MCPGW — OIDC configuration
# Copy to pingone.env and fill in values.
# This file is mounted into the container at /var/lib/procyon/config/pingone.env.
#
# Only read in AGENTLESS (self-hosted frontend) mode — the gateway runs the OIDC
# dance itself and advertises SERVER_URL in its 401 WWW-Authenticate challenge.
# In mesh / cloud-frontend mode the proxy never reads this file (grepping the
# proxy log for these values returns zero hits); see docs/PRIVILEGE-MCP.md.

# Public URL of this MCPGW — the nginx front door, NOT the proxy's -listen port.
# This is what the 401 challenge tells clients to visit, so it must be reachable
# from a browser. Redirect URI to register in PingOne: ${SERVER_URL}/callback
#
# Local Docker Compose (mcpgw-nginx publishes host 443):
SERVER_URL=https://mcpgw.local.ping-devops.com
# On the SE cluster instead use:
# SERVER_URL=https://mcpgw.ai-demo.ping-devops.com

# OIDC application credentials. Environment 8d4d7a4c is the target: it is the only
# environment we hold PingOne Privilege console access in, and the console is where
# the gateway cluster, the MCP Server application's Frontend Name, and the policy
# are authored. No console access means no way to configure the gateway at all.
#
# The gateway signs users in against THIS environment, so its users are the ones who
# reach the MCP tools: cmuir+ssoEndUser@pingone.com and cmuir+ssoAdmin@pingone.com.
# The banking demo's own users live in 01d89b06 and are unrelated to this hop.
# Known app here: deff60f5-5a67-4a6e-b283-47252856c89c.
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret>

# PingOne authorization server endpoints.
OIDC_AUTH_URL=https://auth.pingone.com/8d4d7a4c-de40-4f71-9b98-0c3507cd4d1b/as/authorize
OIDC_TOKEN_URL=https://auth.pingone.com/8d4d7a4c-de40-4f71-9b98-0c3507cd4d1b/as/token
OIDC_USER_URL=https://auth.pingone.com/8d4d7a4c-de40-4f71-9b98-0c3507cd4d1b/as/userinfo

OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile email
